  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia provided technical assistance to the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao (BARMM) to strengthen its social protection systems and policy framework. In line with BARMM&#x2019;s objectives to reduce poverty and build community and family resilience, Australia supported the development of the first BARMM social protection plan (BSPP). BSPP now serves as a foundation for social protection policies in the BARMM. It includes a costed investment plan signalling Bangsamoro Government&#x2019;s commitment to implement the plan. Australia also helped strengthen the BARMM&#x2019;s integrated Beneficiary Database Management System (BDMS). Recommendations are focussed on enhancing the system to digitise data collection and enable digital payments. There are more than 65,000 beneficiaries enrolled in BDMS. Australia&#x2019;s investments are strengthening these foundational systems to ensure improved beneficiary targeting, greater inclusion, and better response to the needs of more people &#x2013; all of which are strengthening the BARMM&#x2019;s social protection accountability.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia worked with the World Bank and the Philippine Maritime Industry Authority (MARINA) to repeal shipping sector regulations that limited foreign ownership. This effectively implemented the 2022 amendments to the Philippine Public Service Act that had lifted foreign ownership restrictions in key public utilities including domestic shipping. This reform will open the sector to new investments, potentially leading to improved services and lower transport costs.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia worked with the World Bank to enhance the Philippines&#x2019; conditional cash transfer program, ensuring it will be able to target assistance to those most in need, including in the aftermath of unexpected economic shocks. Australia also helped improve case management to monitor household well-being and refer families to additional services when needed. Australia supported the national rollout of the Department of Social Welfare and Development&#x2019;s electronic case management system. IECMS is now generating data to inform interventions. Australia also provided technical assistance in the design of the Philippines&#x2019; cash transfer program for children with disabilities.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through UNICEF, has successfully elevated the policy dialogue on introducing a disability-focussed social protection program. The Australian-funded, world-leading Cost of Raising Children with Disabilities study resulted in a draft congressional bill for a new cash transfer program for children with disability.
&#x200d;
Through ADB, Australia piloted a social protection graduation program, linking cash transfers to livelihood support. The program provided business coaching and mentoring; distributed assets; established savings associations; and delivered life skills training. 73% of households reported an increase in monthly income. The successful pilot has influenced DSWD&#x2019;s decision to implement policy reforms and roll out program adaptations nationally.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">DFAT supported the Typhoon Insurance Advisory Project with the International Finance Corporation which developed and deployed a typhoon insurance product for CARD Pioneer Micro-Insurance as the first private sector-led crop insurance in the Philippines. As of June 30, 2022, 37,462 typhoon insurance policies bundled with the agri-loans of three microfinance institutions had been sold in 12 provinces valued at nearly $7 million. With this project, CPMI has helped widen crop insurance in the country by offering the typhoon insurance product to areas not reached by the state-owned Philippine Crop Insurance Corporation. 6,282 farmers have received payouts based on this insurance.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The COVID-19 Waste Management Sourcebook was developed through DFAT&#x2019;s support to the Department of Health&#x2019;s COVID-19 Emergency Response Project (PCERP).  This contains a COVID-19 Waste Management Self-Assessment Compliance Audit Checklist.  The results from the pilot implementation of the online assessment tool involving 400 facilities served as baseline data on health care waste management of facilities in Philippines. Support to PCERP also assisted DOH in the procurement of equipment and COVID-19 vaccines, with savings realised through Bank-facilitated procurement.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the Advancing Multilateral Partnerships for Economic Development (AMPED) program, Australia partnered with the World Bank to fund technical assistance to the Philippine Department of Public Works and Highways (DPWH) to support the preparation and design of the Mindanao Transport Connectivity Improvement Project (MTCIP). MTCIP aims to improve connectivity, climate resilience, and the operational safety of roads in Mindanao. Australia&#x2019;s support for MTCIP included a review of the climate vulnerability of roads and recommendations to strengthen the environmental resilience of road networks within the scope of MTCIP. AMPED also helped DPWH meet environmental and social compliance through the preparation of relevant environmental safeguard instruments and documents. Australia&#x2019;s assistance through AMPED ensured climate resilience was fully integrated into the final design of MTCIP, and in March 2025, the World Bank approved a loan to the Philippine Government to commence the delivery of the road infrastructure improvements in Mindanao.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trade</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the Advancing Multilateral Partnerships for Economic Development (AMPED) program, Australia partnered with the World Bank to provide analytics and advisory services to the Philippine Government to facilitate trade and streamline regulation. Data-market regulatory diagnostics conducted with the Philippine National Privacy Commission scoped regulatory reforms to expand market access for key Philippine service exports, such as offshore business processing. An examination of shipping port systems, including terminal processing, identified possible operational enhancements to increase handling rates and lower trading costs. A policy note identified domestic regulatory barriers that limited competition in the local shipping and air transport sectors, providing the Philippine Government with recommendations to enhance trade competitiveness.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
